<h2 id="vaults">Vaults</h2>
<p>
  A vault is any folder. Its <code>.md</code> files are the notes, and its subfolders are their
  folders. Nothing is added to the folder: no database, and no hidden settings. Names that start
  with a dot, such as <code>.git</code> or <code>.obsidian</code>, are left out. Switch vaults from
  the button at the left of the toolbar, or with File, Switch Vault. Preferences lists your vaults.
  Forget takes one off the list and leaves the folder alone.
</p>
<p>
  New notes get <code>title</code>, <code>created</code>, and <code>tags</code>. The title at the
  top of a note can be edited in place. Enter or clicking away keeps it, and Esc puts it back. A
  note's title is its <code>title</code> property, or else its file name. Changing the title
  renames the file to match. Breadcrumbs above the title show the vault, the folders, and the
  file. View, Breadcrumbs hides that bar. Delete moves a note to the desktop's Trash.
</p>
<p>
  Drag a note or a folder onto a folder to move it, or onto the empty space under the folders to
  move it to the top of the vault. Notes save a second after you stop typing, and with Ctrl+S. A
  note changed by another program is reloaded, or you are asked if you still had unsaved changes.
</p>

<h2 id="properties">Properties</h2>
<p>
  The <code>---</code> block at the top of a note is shown as properties: text, number, checkbox,
  date (with a calendar), list, tags, or link. The type is worked out from the value. Click a
  property's name, or right-click it, to change its type, rename it, or remove it. Add property
  asks for a name and a type. The block folds from its header. <code>title</code> and
  <code>created</code> stay in the file and are not listed there. The file stays plain YAML.
  Nested values are kept as written and shown read-only. Saving writes back only what changed in
  that block. The rest of the file is untouched.
</p>
<p>
  Tags come from the frontmatter (<code>tags: [a, b]</code> or a list) and from <code>#tags</code>
  in the text, outside of code.
</p>

<h2 id="browse">Folders and tags</h2>
<p>
  Browse by folder, in a tree that folds, or by tag. Each tag lists its notes, and there is an
  Untagged group. The sidebar holds those panels. Drag a panel's title onto another panel to make
  them tabs, or to its top or bottom edge to stack them. The same choices are in the menu, in
  View, and in Preferences, Sidebar. Ctrl+T switches tabs, or folds the last pane when they are
  stacked. Search looks at titles, paths, the first line, and tags. <code>#name</code> searches
  tags only. Ctrl+F opens search.
</p>
<p>
  The status bar shows the vault and the open note's word count, and any problem. View, Status Bar
  hides it.
</p>

<h2 id="links">Links</h2>
<p>
  Link a note with <code>[[Note name]]</code>, and optionally <code>[[Note|shown text]]</code> or
  <code>[[Note#Heading]]</code>. A Markdown link to an <code>.md</code> file counts too. A link
  finds its note by path, file name, or title. Typing <code>[[</code> lists matches. Up and Down
  choose, Enter or Tab puts the link in, and Esc hides the list.
</p>
<p>
  Ctrl+click a link in either mode, or click it in live mode, to follow it. A link to a note that
  is not there yet makes it, beside the current note. Peek lists backlinks, with the line each
  link is on, and the open note's own links. Renaming a note updates the <code>[[links]]</code>
  that point at it, and keeps their shown text and heading.
</p>

<h2 id="editing">Editing</h2>
<p>
  Two modes, switched with the button left of the eye at the top right, or from Preferences,
  Editor, View, Live Mode, or Quick Actions. Source mode shows the Markdown as written, colored.
  Live mode still writes Markdown. Every line except the one you are on is rendered, and a heading
  takes its size as soon as its <code>#</code> marks are typed. With the keyboard elsewhere, the
  whole note shows rendered. The reading view (the eye, or Ctrl+Space) works in either mode.
</p>
<div class="pair">
  <Shot
    name="notes-reading-wide.png"
    alt="The reading view of Launch plan, with a code block and a picture"
    caption="Reading view. Links to other notes open in place. Backspace goes back."
  />
  <Shot
    name="notes-source-wide.png"
    alt="Source mode, showing the Markdown of Launch plan in the light theme"
    caption="Source mode, in the light theme. The same note, as written."
  />
</div>
<p>
  The reading view renders headings, bold and italic, lists and tasks, quotes, code blocks with a
  Copy button, tables, rules, links, tags, and pictures stored beside the note. Esc or Ctrl+Space
  closes it. Arrow keys, Page Up and Page Down, Space, Home, and End scroll.
</p>
<p>
  Type <code>/</code> at the start of a line, or after a space, for a menu of blocks: headings 1
  to 3, bulleted and numbered lists, a todo, a super todo when that module is on, a quote, a code
  block, a divider, a link to a note, or today's date. Keep typing to filter. Up and Down choose,
  Enter or Tab adds it, and Esc hides the menu. Used mid-line, a line block starts a new line.
</p>
<Shot
  name="notes-slash-wide.png"
  alt="The slash menu open in a note called Inbox, listing headings and lists"
  caption="The slash menu. Type / at the start of a line."
/>
<p>
  Point at a line and a handle shows in the margin. Drag it up or down. A list item or task brings
  the items nested under it, and a fenced code block moves as a whole. This works in both modes.
</p>
<p>
  Selecting text brings up a small bar: bold, italic, strikethrough, code, link, headings, lists,
  and quote. The same ones are keys. Ctrl+B, Ctrl+I, Shift+Ctrl+X, Ctrl+E, and Ctrl+K wrap the
  selection, or the word under the cursor, and a second press takes the marks off. Ctrl+1, Ctrl+2,
  and Ctrl+3 are headings. Ctrl+0 is plain text. Shift+Ctrl+8, Shift+Ctrl+7, and Shift+Ctrl+9 are
  the lists. Ctrl+Enter ticks a task. Shift+Ctrl+Q is a quote. Shift+Ctrl+E is a code block. They
  are also in the Format menu, the right-click menu, and Preferences, Keyboard.
</p>
<p>
  Drop files from a file manager onto a note. They are copied into the vault's attachments folder
  (<code>Attachments</code> by default, under Preferences, Editor, Attachments) and linked where
  the cursor is, relative to the note. A second <code>photo.png</code> becomes
  <code>photo 2.png</code>. In live mode, a line that is only a picture shows the picture. The
  reading view shows them too.
</p>
<p>
  Preferences, Editor sets three fonts, the editor's size, and its line spacing: Interface, Editor,
  and Monospace. A sample shows the editor's fonts as you choose. The window follows the desktop's
  colors, fonts, and icon theme, in light or dark. Rivet's own icons are the default.
</p>

<h2 id="modules">Modules</h2>
<p>
  Optional, and turned on in Preferences, Modules.
</p>
<p>
  Daily notes are a note for each day, named by its date (<code>2026-11-16.md</code>) in a folder
  you pick. Daily is the default, and the folder is made when it is first needed. Ctrl+D, File,
  Daily Note, or the + button opens today's note, and makes it with a <code>daily</code> tag when
  it is not there yet.
</p>
<p>
  Super todos use a tag, <code>#task</code> by default. A plain <code>- [ ]</code> stays an
  ordinary todo. One that carries the tag is a super todo. Shift+Ctrl+T makes the line at the
  cursor one. The details are plain text in the line:
  <code>due:2026-12-01</code> and <code>priority:high</code>. Progress is the box:
  <code>[ ]</code> to do, <code>[/]</code> in progress, <code>[x]</code> done. In live mode the tag
  folds away, the date becomes a pill, and the priority becomes a flag. Clicking the words opens a
  panel for progress, the due date, the priority, and tags.
</p>
<p>
  The Todos page (the check box button, File, Todos, or Quick Actions) lists every super todo in
  the vault. Filter by text, by Open, In progress, Done, or All, by when it is due, and by tag.
  Sort by due date, priority, or note, which also groups the list. Tick a box to finish a task.
  The note changes on disk. Click a row to open its note. The other view is a board: To do, In
  progress, and Done. Drag a card to another column to change its progress. The List / Board
  switch is remembered. Peek on that page is its own: list or board, width, and counts.
</p>
<div class="pair">
  <Shot
    name="notes-todos-board-wide.png"
    alt="The Todos page as a board, with To do, In progress, and Done columns"
    caption="The Todos page as a board. Drag a card to change its progress."
  />
  <Shot
    name="notes-todos-list-wide.png"
    alt="The Todos page as a list in the light theme, grouped by when each task is due"
    caption="The same page as a list, in the light theme, grouped by due date."
  />
</div>
<Shot
  name="notes-preferences-wide.png"
  alt="Preferences, Modules, with Super todos and Daily notes turned on"
  caption="Preferences, Modules. Both modules are optional."
/>

<h2>Quick Open and Quick Actions</h2>
<p>
  Ctrl+P finds a note from part of its title or folder. Letters in order are enough. With nothing
  typed, it lists the most recently edited notes. Shift+Ctrl+P runs a command or option by name,
  and on / off options are ticked. They use the same panel as Rivet Files.
</p>
<Shot
  name="notes-quick-open-wide.png"
  alt="Quick Open matching Launch plan from the letters la"
  caption="Quick Open. A few letters of the title, then Enter."
/>
<p>
  Peek (the button at the far right of the toolbar, F11, or View, Peek) is a panel about the open
  note: how it is shown, the file's name, folder, size, words, characters, lines, and reading
  time, its dates, and its properties. A width chosen in Peek belongs to that note. Preferences,
  Appearance, Default note width sets it for every other note.
</p>

<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
<ReleaseBlock slug="notes" />
{
  archive && (
    <>
      <p>
        Unpack the archive and run <code>install.sh</code>. That puts <code>rivet-notes</code> in
        <code>~/.local/bin</code> and adds Rivet Notes to the app launcher.
      </p>
      <pre>
        <code>{`tar xzf ${archive.name}\ncd ${archiveFolder(archive.name)}\n./install.sh`}</code>
      </pre>
    </>
  )
}
<p>
  The program is a portable x86_64 build, linked against glibc 2.31. The window needs
  <a href={url("files/#libraries")}>the same libraries as Rivet Files</a>.
</p>
<h3>From source</h3>
<p>
  Rust 1.88 or newer. From the top of the repository,
  <code>utilities/rivet-dev install notes</code> builds a release and adds the launcher entry. Or:
</p>
<pre><code>cargo build --release -p rivet-notes
./target/release/rivet-notes [VAULT]
./target/release/rivet-notes tags [VAULT]
./target/release/rivet-notes --install-desktop-entry</code></pre>

<h2 id="keys">Keys</h2>
<p>The project notes list these:</p>
<dl class="keys">
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd></dt>
    <dd>New note. Shift+Ctrl+N makes a folder.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Save now.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Reading view. Esc closes it.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>F11</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Peek.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Search.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Switch folders and tags, or fold the pane when they are stacked.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Quick Open.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Quick Actions.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Daily note, when that module is on.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>F9</kbd> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Show or hide the sidebar, the menu bar.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>,</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Preferences.</dd>
  </div>
</dl>
<p>
  The docs still list templates, and full-text search in the shared file views, as next. Search
  today looks at titles, paths, the first line, and tags.
</p>
<p>
  Longer notes: <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/rivet-notes.md`}>docs/rivet-notes.md</a>,
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-notes/README.md`}>apps/rivet-notes</a>, and the
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-notes/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a>.
</p>
